/*
gamepadxt.lcb - xTalk Builder (LCB) binding library for game controllers,
                via the gamepad_shim C shim over SDL3. Part of gamepadxt for
                OpenXTalk (OXT).

Once this extension is loaded, every public handler below is callable
directly from xTalk script, e.g.
    if gpStart() then put gpPads() into sPads

Conventions (the shim, src/gamepad_shim.c, is where each is enforced):
  * A pad handle is an integer (0 = invalid). Every handler tolerates a stale
    or 0 handle: getters return 0, false or empty, actions do nothing.
  * NAMES ARE MECHANICAL: the handler for the shim's gpx_pad_set_player is
    gpPadSetPlayer; nothing is renamed by hand. tools/check-lcb-signatures.py
    holds the bijection (every export bound once, every binding wrapped by the
    one public handler of that name).
  * A button or an axis is named by text ("south", "leftx").
  * A result of several values comes back as TEXT, comma-separated, with a
    "." decimal point in every locale; events come one a line. Text results
    cross as a Pointer that textOf() copies, never as ZStringNative. Text
    ARGUMENTS cross as ZStringUTF8, which the engine builds and frees itself.
  * A yes/no crosses the public boundary as a Boolean and the C boundary as
    an int (asInt() below; `tR is not 0` on the way back).
  * Nothing here throws, bar the ABI check: a refused call returns 0, false
    or empty and gpLastError() says why. A read in a frame loop must not be
    able to stop the loop.
  * NOTHING HAPPENS BETWEEN POLLS (suite rule 1): no handler of a script is
    ever called by this library. The script calls gpPoll from a timer and
    reads gpEvents.

The foreign handlers bind to the shared library "gamepadxt" (built by
CMakeLists.txt). Bindings resolve on first use, so the library only needs to
be present at run time.
*/

/**
Game controllers for xTalk: Xbox, PlayStation, Switch and most other pads,
with one button layout, through SDL.

Call <gpStart> once, then <gpPoll> from a timer (every 16 to 33
milliseconds for a game). Each connected controller is a pad handle, an
integer; <gpPads> lists them. Read what is held with <gpPadStick>,
<gpPadAxes> and <gpPadButton>, and read what happened (a button going down,
a controller arriving or leaving) from <gpEvents>. Call <gpStop> when the
stack closes.

Buttons are named by where they are: "south" is the bottom face button on
every pad, whatever is printed on it (<gpButtonNames> lists them all, and
<gpPadButtonLabel> gives the printed letter). A stick axis runs from -1 to
1, with left and up negative; a trigger runs from 0 to 1.

A pad handle stops working when its controller is unplugged, and using one
that no longer works is harmless: getters return 0, false or empty and
actions do nothing. Nothing here throws; <gpLastError> says why a call was
refused.

<gpPadRumble> shakes a pad and <gpPadSetLED> colours its light, where the
pad can. <gpVirtualAttach> makes a pad in software, for on-screen controls
and for testing a game with no controller attached.

Tags: Gamepad, Controller, Games, Library
*/
library org.openxtalk.library.gamepadxt

use com.livecode.foreign

-- The engine's own string builder, resolved by NAME (so no leading
-- underscore): see textOf() below.
foreign handler MCStringCreateWithBytes(in pBytes as Pointer, in pByteCount as CUInt, in pEncoding as CUInt, in pIsExternalRep as CBool, out rString as String) returns CBool binds to "<builtin>"

metadata title is "Gamepads for OpenXTalk"
metadata author is "Seth Morrow Software"
metadata version is "0.1.0"
metadata summary is "Game controllers for xTalk via SDL3: buttons, sticks, triggers, rumble, hot-plug, virtual pads."

